const title       = 'Raven Vision: How Do Ravens See? Colour, UV, and Visual Acuity';
const description = 'Ravens have excellent vision — a key tool for a visually-oriented forager and social species. This page examines raven eyesight including colour vision, UV detection, visual acuity, and how their visual system compares to humans.';
const path        = '/questions/how-do-ravens-see/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'How Do Ravens See?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens see in colour?', a: 'Yes — ravens are tetrachromats, meaning they have four types of cone photoreceptors compared to the human three. This gives ravens colour vision across the visual spectrum including ultraviolet (UV) wavelengths invisible to humans. UV sensitivity is relevant for raven foraging (some food items reflect UV distinctively), feather condition assessment (UV-reflective plumage signals mate quality), and possibly navigation (UV patterns in the sky). Ravens see the world in a broader colour space than humans, though the subjective experience of their colour vision is unknown.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens have good eyesight?', a: 'Ravens have excellent visual acuity — their eyes are proportionally large and have a high density of cone and rod photoreceptors. Like most birds, ravens have a fovea (region of maximum acuity) and some species have a secondary foveal region for forward binocular vision. Ravens can detect food items, conspecifics, and predators at considerable distances. Their visual acuity in photopic (bright light) conditions is estimated to be at least as good as human acuity and likely better for small high-contrast objects.' },
  { q: 'Can ravens see at night?', a: 'Ravens are diurnal (day-active) birds and their eyes are optimised for daylight vision. They are not adapted for low-light hunting as owls are. However, like most corvids, ravens can function in low-light conditions (dawn, dusk, overcast days) better than many other diurnal birds, with sufficient rod photoreceptors for twilight activity. They are not effective foragers in full darkness and roost communally at night rather than remaining active.' },
];
