    <h1>Raven Vision: How Do Ravens See? Colour, UV, and Visual Acuity</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are tetrachromats with four cone types, giving them colour vision extending into ultraviolet wavelengths invisible to humans. Their visual acuity is excellent — they can spot food and social partners at long distances. As diurnal birds, their eyes are optimised for bright-light vision; they are not night-hunters but can function at dusk and dawn.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Tetrachromacy and UV Vision</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Most birds, including corvids, are tetrachromats — they possess four types of cone photoreceptors sensitive to different wavelength ranges: ultraviolet (or violet), short-wave blue, medium-wave green, and long-wave red. Humans are trichromats (three cone types: blue, green, red). This extra UV-sensitive cone gives ravens access to colour information in the UV range (approximately 300–400 nm wavelength) that humans cannot perceive.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>UV vision is functionally relevant for ravens in several contexts:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Food detection:</strong> some food items (urine trails of small mammals, certain insects, some fruits) reflect UV distinctively, potentially allowing ravens to detect food sources invisible to human observers.</li>
      <li><strong>Plumage assessment:</strong> raven feather iridescence has a UV component; UV-reflective plumage may signal individual condition and quality in social contexts including mate choice.</li>
      <li><strong>Navigation:</strong> UV patterns in the sky (the polarisation pattern of skylight, atmospheric scattering) may provide navigational cues used by ravens during long-distance movements.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Visual Acuity</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens' eyes are large relative to body size — a consistent feature of visually-oriented birds. Large eyes allow more light collection and a larger image on the retina, supporting higher spatial resolution. The raven retina contains a fovea — a region of densely packed cone cells providing maximum acuity — in the lateral visual field, appropriate for detection of objects to the side while flying or foraging.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Practical consequences of raven visual acuity:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens can detect a carcass from several hundred metres in open terrain, particularly when other vultures or ravens are already gathering (using conspecifics as distance visual cues).</li>
      <li>They track the gaze direction of other ravens and humans with high precision — a skill relevant to both social monitoring and cache protection.</li>
      <li>They recognise individual human faces reliably, distinguishing between familiar people at considerable distances.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Visual Field and Binocular Vision</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Like most birds, ravens have eyes positioned on the sides of the head, providing a wide monocular visual field (nearly 180° per eye) with a relatively narrow binocular overlap zone in front. This "panoramic" visual arrangement suits animals that need to monitor for predators across a wide field while also processing detail in the forward zone.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Ravens have a blind spot directly behind the head — a vulnerability that may explain some of their characteristic head-turning behaviours (tilting the head to view objects with one eye at close range) and their vigilance behaviour when foraging, turning the head frequently to scan the full visual field.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Vision in Social Contexts</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens' visual sophistication is not purely for finding food — it is also central to their social intelligence. Studies by Bugnyar and colleagues have shown that ravens track the visual access of other ravens and humans with high precision:<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>When caching food, ravens check whether other ravens can see them and modify their caching behaviour if observed.</li>
      <li>They assess whether a potential observer has a clear line of sight — not just whether another bird is present, but whether that bird can specifically see the cache location.</li>
      <li>They recognize when a barrier blocks a competitor's view, using this knowledge to conceal their actions behind objects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This visual perspective-taking — understanding what others can and cannot see — reflects the integration of visual processing with social cognition, making raven vision not just a sensory tool but a component of their social intelligence.</p>
  </section>

